Point tzdata's on-disk lookup at the host zoneinfo directory.

`Data.Time.Zones.Files` is the only module in tzdata that references the
autogenerated `Paths_tzdata` module, and it does so exactly once, to build the
path returned by `timeZonePathFromDB`. Cabal's `Paths_tzdata` hard-codes the
build-time install prefix, so that reference drags six /nix/store paths into
any binary that links it — which is every binary that uses `tz`, because
`Data.Time.Zones.Read` imports this module to implement `loadTZFromDB`
(re-exported from `Data.Time.Zones`).

We never call `loadTZFromDB`: `Seal.Cron.Types.loadCronTZ` uses `loadSystemTZ`
(honours TZDIR, then the system zoneinfo) and falls back to the database
compiled into `Data.Time.Zones.All`. So the value this module returns only
matters if a caller asks for it, and on a machine without Nix the store path it
used to return would not exist anyway.

Rewriting the path to the host zoneinfo directory keeps the module's signature
and makes `loadTZFromDB` actually work on a Nix-less install, rather than
merely removing the string. The TZif payload tzdata ships (`*.zone`) and the
host's files are the same TZif2 format; only the naming differs (`America/
New_York.zone` vs `America/New_York`). It also drops the last /nix/store
dependency from the release artifact, which `scripts/check-portability.sh`
enforces.

--- a/Data/Time/Zones/Files.hs
+++ b/Data/Time/Zones/Files.hs
@@ -10,8 +10,6 @@
   timeZonePathFromDB,
   ) where
 
-import Paths_tzdata
-
 -- | Return the file path corresponding to a time zone location.
 --
 -- Note: doesn't check whether the location is valid (ie. the returned
@@ -17,4 +15,4 @@
 timeZonePathFromDB :: String -> IO FilePath
 timeZonePathFromDB tzName =
   -- TODO(klao): this probably won't work on Windows.
-  getDataFileName $ tzName ++ ".zone"
+  pure $ "/usr/share/zoneinfo/" ++ tzName
